  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1223 PM CDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nWV imagery indicates a west-northwest flow aloft prevailing across \nthe Western High Plains downstream of a flattening ridge axis \nsituated across the Four Corners Region. Near the surface, an area \nof low pressure remains anchored in southeast Colorado.\n\nRelatively dry conditions are likely for much of the area early in \nthe period as the SREF shows an upper level high continuing to \ndominate the Four Corners Region through late Tuesday before \nfinally dislodging eastward into the Central Plains mid-week. \nHowever, a similar set up from yesterday still exists with \nisolated thunderstorms likely developing across the high plains\nof eastern Colorado late afternoon as yet another upper level \nshortwave ejects out of the northern Colorado Rockies. Sufficient\nmid-level moisture/instability present combined with steepening\nmid-level lapse rates will set the stage for thunderstorm \ndevelopment as ejecting H5 vort maxima interact with a trough \naxis/stalled boundary in northeast Colorado. A few stray storms \ndrifting into west central and extreme southwest Kansas cannot \nbe ruled out later this evening as suggested by some CAMs. After\na mainly dry Tuesday, very minimal thunderstorm chances (20%) \nreturn to west central/extreme southwest Kansas late Wednesday \nas an upper level shortwave trough climbs northeast across the \nhigh plains of eastern Colorado on the backside of a ridge axis \ndeparting through the Central Plains.\n\nAnother warm night is expected tonight as prevailing southerlies \ncontinue to reinforce a warmer air mass across southwest/central \nKansas. With the HREF showing a widespread 70-90% probability of \ntemperatures dropping below 80F, look for lows mainly down into the \n70s(F). Well above normal temperatures are likely Tuesday and \nWednesday with ridging aloft transitioning slowly east through the \nregion and southerlies prevailing across southwest/central Kansas, \nsupporting H85 temperatures a little above 30C in central Kansas to \nnear 35C near the Colorado line. Expect afternoon highs up around to \na little above 100F both days with the HREF painting a widespread 70-\n90% probability of temperatures exceeding 100F.",
